ne of the other ones go first and then  i'll play into it  okay thank you yeah  so i think it's really important not to  um you know not to make everybody work  in the time zone of the headquarters  right i mean i think we have to get used  to this model where people are working  during their preferred daytime working  hours in their time zone  and i understand the need for some  synchronous communication and some  overlap  but um i think we just have to figure  out a way to make it you know two or  three hours of overlap or whatever the  minimum overlap needed uh for teams to  communicate across time zones because  this is just one of those  long-term unsustainable things to do is  to have you know a  certain team work in a completely  different time zone and i think as  leaders it's very important to model  that and enforce that right and you know  to create opportunities such that  uh you know  an extended amount of overlap is not  necessary  and when i say modeling uh behavior like  if  leaders start attending meetings and you  know early morning or answering emails  after work hours or in the weekends  it  it indirectly creates that you know  expectation or that culture that oh like  okay maybe this is like the work ethic  this is this demonstrates work ethic  like being able to you know extend  oneself and you know cut into you know  devote their personal time but which is  which really is not i mean i think it  really sets a bad example and i think it  you know uh as leaders  they have uh that opportunity to set the  culture for the rest of uh their  organization and the company and they  should  um you know certainly  model that behavior  actually that plays into some of the  things that i've heard for one thing the  research at the be  in the first year of the shutdown was  the fact that people were  more productive  that were working from home  but also that they were answering they  could get up you know maybe eat  breakfast and that kind of thing it's  kind of split their day instead of  normally we drive to work  and then at five o'clock we close the  computer and go home but now some people  were oh okay well it's kind of calm here  in the evening i'll answer some emails  and they were answering emails at nine  or ten o'clock  but the thing is  yes  worry about  burnout that's for sure but also that it  does give people kind of  the chance to spread it out spread out  their work day kind of manage family and  everything else and so that was working  out rather nicely  i was going to give an analogy for me  about overworking and even  burnout  risk which i think is um if you see a  group of boys  who are you're taking a photo of a group  of boys it tends to be boys i think  one of them will stand up you to be a  bit taller  and then another one will try to do the  same and very quickly all the boys try  to stand up to be looking taller and  then everyone is at the same height on  the photo as it would have been if no  one is standing up but everyone is  suffering from trying to stand up taller  and i feel like with everyone moving  remote and saving time on commute you  end up with people working even harder  and then at some point you actually  can't even control it you don't even  realize you're standing up taller for  way too long  and so i think  overworking and burnout risk is true  even in face to face even before the  pandemic  and it's very hard to identify it's even  harder to do remote  and and  i can speak for myself because we just  had last week our second face to face  for all the team  and i think there are two of my team  members  i think are overworking to the point  where they could risk burning out and  one reports directly to me so i i'm  taking steps but this is really really  hard to identify when you just meet in  zoom and the person is smiling and you  can't really tell  so i  i'm looking for for answers to this  actually but but the way i'm looking at  it is this group of people who are  t