tion i'll see if any of our  panelists if there's anything you would  like to share um that stood out to you  from your experience in the program and  and from uh kind of being a part of it  yeah i can uh answer so i think the  program really helped me to recognize my  own blind spots of uh you know that i  was that i had when leading from home  uh so the example that i shared about  trusting my team more empowering them to  make more decisions and not not being  the bottleneck you know which which does  not mean that i'm not really you know  doing my part in you know my job or  enabling the decision making it just  means that i'm building a high trust  team and how do i do that without being  the bottleneck without without being the  gatekeeper um so that particular thing i  i think the program really helped me  recognize my own blind spots  i think the way i like to think about  the program  and my key takeaway is you have no one  size fits all  and and having maybe a bit provocative  but there's a lot of value from the  teachers from stanford  but actually the value from the  practitioners that were all students and  sharing with each other was really  really helpful so because you're not  talking about any practitioners you're  talking about practitioners who decided  to spend time to learn about the best  practices of leading from home and  hybrid mode and so on and so you have  this group of people who really want to  better  their own understanding and do well for  their team  that's a special group  i agree i like the  the flexibility i like the coffee chats  too that was kind of fun to show up to  that just to get some free conversation  and it was it was really  very interesting but i think it was  just getting leaders to think about new  norms about being flexible  about figuring out who your people are  and  ways to like you were mentioning  ronnie about the  the um  breakout rooms that we did during the  session and you get to know other people  when th