 that we're making  so that's kind of my personal take  although there certainly are some unique  elements that we also do cover in the  program um any any um other reflections  as a parting thoughts from uh from our  panelists  yeah i agree with uh what you just said  i think a lot of things that were just  you know taken for granted uh in the  past you know we just have to be a bit  more intentional about that in the in  the remote world but yeah i mean yeah in  general yes the best practices are  applied to both in person and remote but  it just needs that extra bit of uh  intentionality  yeah  i saw a lot of questions about tips and  tricks  and and i and this is back to my  conclusion that it's no not one size  fits all  but there's one thing that we  implemented which i think is very useful  for the audience which is around the  hybrid model  and at some point we started to have  hybrid where some people were in the  meeting and some people were remote and  we realized that people were together  start to look at each other and  completely ignore people who are remote  and so what we've done is we've enforced  that even when you meet in the same room  everyone has their own laptop looking at  their webcam and connecting with  everyone else but being much more  inclusive and i thought i was being too  harsh to my team to do that and then i  watched a youtube interview with sequoia  saying that they had the exact same  process and so when we were thinking  about hybrid and inclusion for everyone  including remote i really recommend this  tip of everyone even if you're in the  same meeting room  in the same space  look into your laptop and engage with  everyone even remote  so leveling leveling the playing field  in that way um so so wonderful great to  end with a concrete tip and suggestion i  want to thank all of you again for  joining us today both you the audience  that have spent some time with us uh  today as well as our panelists who have  uh taken some tim